<% content_for :title, "About" %>
<p class="eyebrow">FIELD GUIDE / PROJECT NOTES</p>
<h1>About this Pokédex</h1>
<p class="lead">A Rails catalog of the original 151 Pokémon and their types as recorded by PokéAPI.</p>

<div class="row g-4 mt-2">
  <section class="col-lg-6">
    <h2>Data sources</h2>
    <p>The seed script reads three bundled extracts from the <%= link_to "PokéAPI CSV collection",
      "https://github.com/PokeAPI/pokeapi/tree/master/data/v2/csv" %>.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt><code>pokemon.csv</code></dt>
      <dd>Default entries with source IDs 1–151 supply names, height in decimeters, weight in hectograms, and base experience.</dd>
      <dt><code>types.csv</code></dt>
      <dd>Type IDs and names supply the elemental types used by those entries.</dd>
      <dt><code>pokemon_types.csv</code></dt>
      <dd>Pokémon and type IDs pair each entry with its primary or secondary type.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>The catalog uses Pokémon HOME sprites from the <%= link_to "PokéAPI sprite collection",
      "https://github.com/PokeAPI/sprites/tree/master/sprites/pokemon/other/home" %>.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="col-lg-6">
    <h2>How the data connects</h2>
    <p>The importer uses the CSV IDs to match records. Rails assigns its own primary keys, and the join table stores those keys alongside the type slot.</p>
    <p>One Pokémon can have up to two types. One type can belong to many Pokémon. The type pages and search dropdown use that same relationship.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="col-12">
    <h2>Database diagram</h2>
    <img class="erd" src="/erd.svg" width="960" height="280" alt="Each Pokémon and each elemental type can have many Pokémon typings. A Pokémon typing belongs to exactly one Pokémon and one elemental type.">
  </section>
</div>
